# Written here rather than committed so the bytes are the ones the case means.
#
# Two of them are the reason make-oracle-recording-trailing-newline was filed:
# `no-newline` ends without a terminator and `only-newline` is nothing but one,
# and a reader that put a newline back on every non-empty file could record
# neither as itself. They were hidden behind a leading dot until that was fixed;
# they are ordinary files now, and the recording holds their bytes.
printf 'a\n' > one-newline
printf 'a\n\n' > two-newlines
printf 'a\n\n\n' > three-newlines
printf 'a' > no-newline
printf '\n' > only-newline
printf '' > empty
